WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Permissions define what access an application requests or receives.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:12.280
In Microsoft identity platform terms, delegated permissions apply when an application acts on behalf of a signed-in user.

3
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:20.140
Application permissions, often represented as app roles for a resource, can allow app-only access without a signed-in user.

4
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.200
Those two models have different review requirements and different operational impact.

5
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:30.920
Scopes should represent clear API permissions and should be enforced by the resource API.

6
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:37.100
A scope name in a token is not useful unless the API checks it before performing the protected action.

7
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.040
App roles can represent application-level permissions or user-assigned roles depending on the design.

8
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.700
They also need server-side enforcement and review.

9
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.940
Consent is a security and governance decision, not a checkbox to make code work.

10
00:00:51.040 --> 00:01:00.960
User consent, admin consent, tenant-wide consent, and customer onboarding all affect who has approved access and what data or operations the application may reach.

11
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:06.580
Admin consent deserves special review because it can authorize organization-wide access.

12
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.540
Developers should request the least privilege needed for the actual scenario.

13
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.760
A narrow permission set is easier to explain, monitor, approve, and revoke.

14
00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:24.260
Microsoft Graph permissions vary widely in sensitivity, so broad Graph permissions should receive extra scrutiny and clear justification.

15
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:33.020
Permission review should include the resource API, client app, tenant administrators, security owners, and production operators.

16
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.920
The question is not only whether the application can get a token.

17
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:44.740
The question is whether the access represented by that token is appropriate, enforced, observable, and still needed.
